|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_project_i_and_j_

    What are the orthogonal (= perpendicular) 
    projections of the
    vectors $(1, 0)$, $(0, 1)$ 
    onto a $(-1, 1)$-ruler
    placed at angle $\theta$ in the unit circle?

    |> Image
        src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_statement.svg

    (I.e., find the 
    ruler-readings
    marked ‘1’ and ‘2’ above
    as a function of the angle $\theta$.)

    |> Solution

        _Solution 1._ If we rotate the diagram by $-\theta$
        to align the ruler with the $x$ axis the new angles
        of the vectors are $-\theta$ and $\eta - \theta$
        (changed from $0$ and $\eta$, respectively):
        
        |> Image
            src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_sol1.svg

        The ruler-readings are therefore
        $$
        \cos(-\theta) = \cos(\theta)
        $$
        and
        $$
        \cos(\eta - \theta) = \sin(\theta)
        $$

        respectively (in order ‘1’, ‘2’), making use of
        basic identities. (See the section “Identities with $\eta$”
        in this chapter.)

        |> Pause

        _Solution 2._ 
        Recalling first...

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ch_unit_circle_with_new_definition.svg

        ...this figure from the chapter, we note that
        there is an alternate
        set of definitions for sin and cos that is 
        based on clockwise instead of counterclockwise rotation:
        
        |> Image
            src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_sol2.svg

        Now comparing this set of definitions to
        this figure...

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_sol3.svg

        ...in which, by contrast to usual, angles are measured 

        __positively clockwise__

        (that's why it's “$\theta$” not “$-\theta$”) 
        it becomes trivial to see that the two ruler-readings
        are just $\cos(\theta)$ (‘1’) and $\sin(\theta)$ (‘2’).

        |> Pause

        _Solution 3._ 
        More generally, when a unit
        vector is projected onto a “unit ruler”,
        we can switch the ruler _&_ vector positions
        without affecting the ruler-reading:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_sol4.svg

        (Because what counts is the angle between the
        “forward end” of the ruler and the forward end of
        the vector, measured as an angle between $0^\circ$
        and $180^\circ$, and this angle remains the same.)
        !! If we apply this to the problem statement, twice,
        !! the unique ruler is replaced by a unique vector,
        !! while the two vectors are each replaced by a ruler:
        If we apply this to the problem statement
        the unique ruler is replaced by a unique vector
        while the two vectors are each replaced by a ruler:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_sol5_v2.svg

        ...and in the new configuration, at bottom right, it is 
        !! ...and, in this new configuration, it becomes 
        trivial to see 
        that the answers are $\cos(\theta)$ (‘1’),
        $\sin(\theta)$ (‘2’)!
